1
00:00:04,250 --> 00:00:08,930
Pulse features. The first stage of every request, taken apart in depth.

2
00:00:09,730 --> 00:00:16,501
Features are the first stage of every request. They run before any filter, on every record in the cohort.

3
00:00:17,301 --> 00:00:25,565
Each feature adds new columns. A later feature can build on an earlier one, like a bucketize that reads the log column just made.

4
00:00:26,365 --> 00:00:38,234
And every later stage can read those columns, filters included. Attributes, by default, run after the filters. The previous-state attribute is the exception: it runs before them.

5
00:00:39,034 --> 00:00:47,469
Pulse ships a growing set of feature operators, and more are added over time. Your instance's manifest always lists the ones it offers.

6
00:00:48,269 --> 00:00:55,744
Features come in two cost classes. Per-row features need only the record in front of them, so they finish in a single pass.

7
00:00:56,544 --> 00:01:05,405
Global-pass features need the whole cohort first: category counts, target means or quantile cut points. Pulse sweeps once to learn them.

8
00:01:06,205 --> 00:01:09,434
Then it rewinds and emits a value for every record.

9
00:01:10,234 --> 00:01:15,149
Both classes can stream. A global pass just costs a second read of the file.

10
00:01:15,949 --> 00:01:27,733
With one exception: when a global pass learns from a column an earlier feature made, such as quantiles over the log of income, the request runs buffered. Predict says so, and says why.

11
00:01:28,533 --> 00:01:36,861
Many real measures have a long right tail: lots of small values and a few huge ones. That skew distorts averages and models.

12
00:01:37,661 --> 00:01:45,242
Square root is the gentle fix. A negative value becomes null rather than an error, so the rest of the cohort keeps going.

13
00:01:46,042 --> 00:01:54,882
Log goes further. Pulse uses the log of one plus x, so zero stays zero, and anything at or below minus one becomes null.

14
00:01:55,682 --> 00:02:03,285
Bucketize turns a number into a band. With explicit boundaries, each value lands in a numbered bucket, in a single pass.

15
00:02:04,085 --> 00:02:11,794
A value exactly on a cut point goes to the lower bucket. The output is the bucket number, not a label, and null stays null.

16
00:02:12,594 --> 00:02:21,882
Or ask for quantiles, and Pulse learns cut points that give every bucket the same number of records. You choose boundaries or quantiles, never both.

17
00:02:22,682 --> 00:02:30,263
Categories need encoding before a model can use them. One-hot gives every category its own zero-or-one column.

18
00:02:31,063 --> 00:02:38,623
The columns come from the schema's dictionary, so even a category with no records gets one. A null row is all zeros.

19
00:02:39,423 --> 00:02:48,327
A packed boolean works as a two-level category: one-hot gives it member zero and member one, and the encoders and stratify accept it too.

20
00:02:49,127 --> 00:02:58,074
Frequency encoding replaces each category with its share of the cohort. Features run before filters, so those shares count every record.

21
00:02:58,874 --> 00:03:04,301
Target encoding replaces a category with the average outcome of the records that share it.

22
00:03:05,101 --> 00:03:12,469
Smoothing pulls rare categories toward the training mean. A category seen only five times shouldn't get an extreme score.

23
00:03:13,269 --> 00:03:26,845
But there's a trap. Features run before filters, so without fit-on, every average includes every record's outcome: test rows, and the record's own. A split alone changes nothing, and Pulse warns of the leakage.

24
00:03:27,645 --> 00:03:37,445
So name the training rows, with fit-on: split equals zero. Only those rows feed the averages, every row is still encoded, and the warning goes quiet.

25
00:03:38,245 --> 00:03:45,400
Add folds and a random seed, and each training row is encoded from the other folds, so its own outcome never counts.

26
00:03:46,200 --> 00:03:52,160
Date features split one date into calendar parts: year, month, day, day of week, and quarter.

27
00:03:52,960 --> 00:03:58,493
Day of week is ISO: Monday is one, and Sunday is seven, counted on the local day.

28
00:03:59,293 --> 00:04:10,821
A datetime adds the hour, and is read on the local clock. Name a zone column with tz-field, and each row is read in its own zone, so one instant lands on different days.

29
00:04:11,621 --> 00:04:17,240
A row with no zone gets nulls in every date column, never a quiet UTC fallback.

30
00:04:18,040 --> 00:04:30,656
Pulse looks for a zone in order: the slot's own zone, its zone field, the request's zone field, the request's zone, the instance default, then UTC. A plain date has no zone, so it never shifts.

31
00:04:31,456 --> 00:04:39,848
Polynomial features add powers of a number: its square, its cube, up to the tenth power. The original column stays as it is.

32
00:04:40,648 --> 00:04:48,763
A straight line misses a curve. Give a linear regression the squared column too, and it follows the bend. That's polynomial regression.

33
00:04:49,563 --> 00:04:55,694
Watch the scale. The tenth power of one hundred is ten to the twentieth. Standardise the column first.

34
00:04:56,494 --> 00:05:03,606
Train-test split tags every record with a partition: zero for train, one for validation, two for test.

35
00:05:04,406 --> 00:05:12,222
Stratify by a category, or a packed boolean, and each class is split in the same proportions, so the partitions stay balanced.

36
00:05:13,022 --> 00:05:21,030
The same seed and the same rows always give the same split. Downstream, a filter on split zero keeps just the training rows.

37
00:05:21,830 --> 00:05:29,326
Here's a full preparation pipeline in one request. Features run in the order you list them, so each can build on the last.

38
00:05:30,126 --> 00:05:38,518
The log of income feeds a decile bucketize. Region becomes indicator columns, and the signup date becomes calendar parts.

39
00:05:39,318 --> 00:05:46,878
The target encoder comes after the split, with fit-on: split equals zero, so only training rows shape its averages.

40
00:05:47,678 --> 00:06:01,574
Predict checks the whole pipeline from the header, before a single record is read. It finds no leakage, and it says the request buffers, because the decile bucketize and the encoder both read columns that earlier features made.

41
00:06:02,374 --> 00:06:10,553
That's the feature toolkit today: reshape a number, bin it, encode a category, split a date, add curvature, or partition the cohort.

42
00:06:11,353 --> 00:06:19,660
New feature operators are added over time, and embedders can register their own. The manifest always shows what your instance offers.

43
00:06:20,460 --> 00:06:25,652
Remember the one rule: features run first, so they see everything. Next up, filters.

44
00:06:26,452 --> 00:06:27,761
Pulse features.
